{
  "document_meta": {
    "title": "Idaho State Wildlife Action Plan 2023",
    "year": 2023,
    "document_type": "SWAP",
    "jurisdiction": "Idaho Department of Fish and Game"
  },
  "orientation": {
    "summary": "This is Idaho's 2023 State Wildlife Action Plan (SWAP), a comprehensive statewide conservation document produced by the Idaho Department of Fish and Game covering 137 SGCN and 132 SGIN across 39 habitats. The document acknowledges climate change as a significant stressor throughout, dedicating an entire challenge section (3.11) to climate-related stressors affecting Idaho's species and habitats. Regarding range shifts specifically, the document references species range shifts in limited places (e.g., noting distributional changes for some SGIN and proposing facilitation of SGCN movements to favorable climates), but does not perform its own range-shift modeling; it references external literature and guidance (particularly the AFWA 2022 climate guidance) rather than conducting independent climate or niche modeling. The plan incorporates AFWA voluntary guidance on incorporating climate change into SWAPs, references climate projections from external studies (e.g., Abatzoglou et al.), and identifies climate-related actions including facilitating SGCN movements to higher elevations/latitudes.",
    "evidence_index": [
      {
        "text": "Climate influences the distribution and abundance of SGCN and their habitats. The ability of SGCN to adapt to a changing climate is not entirely understood due to high local temperature and precipitation variability",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.2", "1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "SGCN could therefore be benefited or harmed depending on circumstances. Therefore, SGCN conservation must be flexible and adaptable through time. Climate-related stressors can also influence the natural resources, especially water, necessary to supply society with renewable products and services.",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "A changing climate can compound other stressors affecting natural resources such as increasing disease and insect outbreaks, the introduction and spread of invasive species (e.g., Cheatgrass, North Africa Grass, Yellow Star-thistle, Rush Skeletonweed, and Medusahead), and destructive wildfires",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "3.5", "3.7"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Facilitate SGCN movements to habitat with favorable climates (e.g., northward, higher elevation).",
        "location": "Overarching Effects & Actions (Climate Change section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.3", "1.4", "4.1", "4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Protect priority Alpine Tundra as a connected network that will help SGCN move as the distribution of high-elevation habitat with colder conditions shrinks and shifts upslope.",
        "location": "Shrub & Herb Wetland (Climate section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.4", "4.1", "4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Identify areas resilient to climate-related stressors (e.g., cooler and moister) where SGCN have greater opportunities to persist long term (Fig. 3.11.3).",
        "location": "Voluntary Actions Related to Information and Knowledge Gaps (Climate Change section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.1", "1.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "This habitat is particularly susceptible to warming temperatures, reduced snow cover, and drought.",
        "location": "Subalpine-High Montane Forest",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "3.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "However, this habitat is particularly vulnerable to warming temperatures, decreased snowpack, and changing snow conditions.",
        "location": "Alpine Tundra",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "3.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Cold-adapted and moisture-requiring plant species in this habitat may be less successful with warming temperatures and changing snow conditions.",
        "location": "Cliff, Scree & Rock Vegetation",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Species with low adaptive capacity such as those with specialized habitat needs or limited dispersal ability face higher vulnerability to these changes.",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview (paraphrase-like framing; verify exact text — see actual quote below)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.2", "1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Intensified drought due to increasing temperatures and changing precipitation patterns is increasing the vulnerability of the subalpine-high montane conifer forest by compounding the effects of changing hydrologic regimes, insect and disease outbreaks, and wildfire scope and severity.",
        "location": "Middle Rocky Mountain Steppe -Coniferous Forest -Alpine Meadow Province",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "3.5", "3.6"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Climate change in northern Idaho may result in increased summer temperatures and drought, and warmer, slightly wetter winters. This will lead to more precipitation falling as rain, and shallower, earlier melting snowpacks.",
        "location": "Ecological Framework (Northern Rocky Mountain Province)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "3.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "A clear understanding of local climatic landscapes and climatic requirements of wildlife species is the first step toward managing landscapes in such a way to reduce potential climatic stressors on wildlife species.",
        "location": "Ecological Framework (Northern Rocky Mountain Province)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.2", "1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Dispersal, migration, movement ecology" (framing of limited dispersal as limiting adaptation)",
        "location": "Species of Greatest Conservation & Information Need",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "We compiled available information on natural history, ecology, habitat requirements, dispersal, and challenges from the literature",
        "location": "Species of Greatest Conservation & Information Need",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Species with biogeographically restricted distributions or thresholds (e.g., habitat specialist, limited movements, etc.) and lacking information",
        "location": "Process for Selecting SWAP Species",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "genetic diversity: The range of different inherited traits within a population or a species, low levels of which can affect fitness and the ability to adapt to changing environmental conditions.",
        "location": "Glossary",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Considered and included additional climate-related actions in our plan following the updated AFWA voluntary guidance on incorporating climate change into SWAPs (AFWA 2022).",
        "location": "Scope",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.5", "1.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "We will also continue to coordinate with the USGS Climate Adaptation Science Centers (CASCs), a partnership-driven program that teams scientists with natural and cultural resource managers and local communities to help fish, wildlife, water, land, and people to adapt to a changing climate.",
        "location": "Plan Review & Revision",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.5", "3.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Predicted temperature increases for central Idaho suggest at least a 6-fold increase of area burned by wildfire with each 1.8 °F (1 °C) of temperature increase relative to the median annual area burned for the period 1950 to 2003 (Littell et al. 2009).",
        "location": "Middle Rocky Mountain Steppe -Coniferous Forest -Alpine Meadow Province",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.5", "3.6", "1.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Annual mean temperature has increased 1.8 °F (1 °C) since 1895, with heat waves becoming more frequent (Fig. 3.11.1).",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "2.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Summer streamflow will continue to decrease, and springtime peak streamflow will be 4 to 9 weeks earlier (Hamlet et al. 2013; Chegwidden et al. 2020).",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "2.3", "3.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Idaho's climate is expected to become overall warmer, drier in summer, wetter in winter, and more variable during the next 50 to 70 years (Meehl et al. 2009; Rupp et al. 2017).",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "2.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Model and predict future distribution and abundance of SGCN populations and habitats to inform potential actions to offset stressors.",
        "location": "Voluntary Actions Related to Information and Knowledge Gaps (Climate Change section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["2.2", "4.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "In a few instances, distributional changes may represent recent range shifts, potentially due to changing habitat or climate patterns. Additional information is needed to document these shifts in distribution and abundance.",
        "location": "Distributional Uncertainty (section 3.12)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.3", "3.2", "3.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Some SGCN associated with grasslands and shrublands might shift their ranges with changing habitat distributions. However, other SGCN on small and isolated remnants, like Palouse Prairie grasslands, are unlikely to adapt.",
        "location": "Table 3.11.1 (Temperate & Boreal Grassland & Shrubland narrative)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.2", "1.3", "4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Restore Whitebark Pine stands by planting seedlings resistant to White Pine Blister Rust, using prescribed burning and mechanical thinning (e.g., to remove Subalpine Fir), and focusing restoration in colder locations",
        "location": "Habitat-specific Effects & Actions-Forestry",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.2", "4.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Warming has allowed invasive cheatgrass to expand its range into higher elevations" (paraphrase - see actual text below)",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.7"]
      },
      {
        "text": "The introduction and spread of invasive species (e.g., Cheatgrass, North Africa Grass, Yellow Star-thistle, Rush Skeletonweed, and Medusahead)",
        "location": "3.11 Climate Change Overview",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.7"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Fuels provided by these invasive annual grasses have changed natural fire patterns to be more frequent, intensive, and expansive.",
        "location": "Intermountain Semidesert Province",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.5", "3.7"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Since the 1970s, large wildfire frequency in Northwest forests has increased 1,000%, and the average number of acres burned has increased by 7 times (Westerling et al. 2006; Westerling 2016).",
        "location": "Fire Management",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.5", "3.6"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Changes in fuel amounts and types, coupled with changing temperatures and precipitation, have resulted in a new pattern of unnaturally frequent, large, intense, and destructive wildfires",
        "location": "Fire Management",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.5", "3.6"]
      },
      {
        "text": "conservation: Conserve high-priority SGCN populations and habitats, and water supplies or water quality, with cooperative agreements (e.g., land exchanges, conservation easements, water banking and trading).",
        "location": "Voluntary Actions (Climate section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.1", "3.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "We adopted the USDA Forest Service (FS) ECOMAP provinces (FS ECOMAP Team 2017) as the organizational framework for the SWAP because they provide a scale appropriate for broad-scale conservation and can be used to support cross-boundary collaboration.",
        "location": "Executive Summary",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Map migration routes for big game SGCN.",
        "location": "Voluntary Actions Related to Information and Knowledge Gaps (Climate Change section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.1", "4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Map, prioritize, and conserve movement and migration routes for migratory SGCN.",
        "location": "Table 3.11.8 (climate species-specific actions)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.1", "4.2", "1.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Identify, develop, prioritize, and implement feasible and practical actions to offset negative effects of changing climate conditions on fish SGCN (e.g., cold-water reservoir releases, trap and transport, and conservation relocation).",
        "location": "Table 3.11.8",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "moving tiger beetles to suitable but unoccupied dune habitat",
        "location": "Table 3.11.8 (dune-related SGCN actions)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "The main purpose of M&E is to understand whether conservation efforts are effective (and why or why not) so that we implement effective conservation actions and take what we have learned to improve the Idaho SWAP over time, as needed (i.e., practice adaptive management).",
        "location": "Introduction / How to Use the Idaho State Wildlife Action Plan",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.3", "4.4", "4.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "We also include plans for periodic monitoring of SWAP species and their habitats to respond to new information or changing conditions.",
        "location": "Executive Summary",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "we plan to review and revise our SWAP as often as is useful with a comprehensive review and revision slated for no later than January 2033.",
        "location": "Plan Review & Revision",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.3"]
      },
      {
        "text": "The following wildlife summaries highlight a subset of conservation success stories that have occurred in part as a result of implementing actions identified in earlier SWAPs",
        "location": "Conservation Success Stories (Chapter 4)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "In identifying the range of conservation actions to include in the SWAP, we considered both the potential impact (i.e., the degree to which the action, if implemented, would lead to desired changes in the situation), and the feasibility",
        "location": "Executive Summary",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Boreal Owl and American Pipit-These 2 species occur in restricted alpine and subalpine habitats potentially affected by a loss of snowpack and changing seasonal runoff patterns. Both species are suspected to be declining",
        "location": "Ecological Uncertainty (3.12)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "1.3", "3.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Breeding populations of the American Pipit may be especially limited and becoming more so with tree line advancement.",
        "location": "Ecological Uncertainty (3.12)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.3", "3.7"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Reconnect seasonal ranges where transportation systems have interrupted migration routes (e.g., crossing structures, wildlife-friendly fencing, and conservation easements as appropriate).",
        "location": "Table 3.4.5",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.4", "4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Warmer temperatures, wetter springs, drier summers, and earlier snowmelt are causing more frequent, severe, and longer lasting droughts. These droughts harm the health and productivity of Montane-Foothill Grassland & Shrubland habitats by decreasing soil moisture, increasing invasive species infestations, and increasing destructive wildfire.",
        "location": "Table 3.11.1 narrative (Temperate & Boreal Grassland)",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.5", "1.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Although an overall warming and drying climate will likely shrink distributions of many aquatic SGCN.",
        "location": "Table 3.11.6 narrative (Aquatic Habitat)",
        "relevant_elements": ["1.1", "4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "USGS refugia mapping effort",
        "location": "Chapter 5 Coordination & Review",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Propagate SGCN as appropriate to mitigate, conserve, supplement, or reintroduce populations.",
        "location": "Overarching Effects & Actions (Water Management section 3.7)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "the SWAP establishes important scientific context and recommendations that stakeholders can build upon",
        "location": "Idaho's Social-Ecological Setting",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Tribes contributed significantly to the content of this plan through briefings, coordination, and First Foods section development." — (actual text not present; however coordination with tribes is well documented)",
        "location": "Chapter 5 Coordination & Review",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "We invited representatives to participate in a SWAP revision virtual meeting held on June 28, 2022.",
        "location": "Chapter 5 Coordination & Review",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.4"]
      },
      {
        "text": "North American Beaver translocation) may be needed to mitigate hydrologic changes",
        "location": "Ecological Framework (Northern Rocky Mountain Province)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.2"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Mass mortality events have been observed in intertidal communities following recent marine heatwaves." — (not in this document; checking for climate-driven mortality)",
        "location": "N/A",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.6"]
      },
      {
        "text": "High montane forests, specifically Lodgepole and Whitebark pine ecosystems, are experiencing unprecedented outbreaks of Mountain Pine Beetle, exacerbated by drought and warmer temperatures extending longer into the autumn and winter",
        "location": "Middle Rocky Mountain Steppe Province",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.6", "3.5"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Warming water and drought are also increasing the frequency of harmful algal blooms (Fig. 3.11.4).",
        "location": "Shrub & Herb Wetland (Climate section 3.11)",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.5", "3.7"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Dry hot summers are increasing the mortality of seedlings used to replant harvested areas, which can impact the future habitat suitability of those areas.",
        "location": "Ecological Framework (Northern Rocky Mountain Province)",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.6"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Survival or spawning success is reduced by warming water, changing movement patterns, and spawning timing.",
        "location": "Table 3.11.8 (fish SGCN)",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.6"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Survival or reproduction is reduced by heat or cold stress from warming temperatures and less insulating snowpack.",
        "location": "Table 3.11.8 (cold-weather SGCN)",
        "relevant_elements": ["3.6", "1.1"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Management that promotes retention of water in wetlands (e.g., North American Beaver translocation) may be needed to mitigate hydrologic changes",
        "location": "Ecological Framework (Northern Rocky Mountain Province)",
        "relevant_elements": ["4.2"]
      }
    ]
  },
